title The argv named once boots twice -- the QEMU condense lands behavior-proven, and the destaging hand finds its own name
prompt The in-flight tools/co analysis returned; the fire movement's last granted door executes as its coda -- no new kg, no new rota row, the prior baton's own promise kept.
think The analysis pinned the language facts from the interpreter source: Rishi has file-local fn (one-expression body, bounds far clear), no cross-file include, and run/spawn accept a fn-built argv list -- with one landmine, that a spawn inside a fn body registers its handle in the call's copied Env and strands the caller's wait-for. So the fns RETURN argv lists and the caller spawns. The exact four insert lines were proven on the real interpreter by a fresh probe (including the one composition the first probe missed, an interpolated run inside a fn body) before any orchestrator moved.
think The transformer then hoisted by the almanac method: extract each site, REGENERATE the elder line from the pinned template, refuse the file on any byte difference. All 15 files passed with 135 sites and zero flags -- 67 build sites and 68 QEMU sites, exactly two QEMU templates with the timeout role-determined. Applied: every long template now stands once per file as a fn, call sites read let rx = spawn (qemu_rx sock "receipt-rx.elf"), and the family falls about 9.8KB with the teaching comment living once in the device lab and fourteen one-line pointers home.
think Then the proof this bench could uniquely give: qemu-system-riscv64 is installed here, so two labs ran end-to-end through every condensed layer -- shim to runner to fn-hoisted orchestrator to real riscv64 guests over a virtio wire. Receipt (single-sock class) answered its sealed-receipt GREEN; murr (multi-sock class, two cables) answered mint plus receipt GREEN. The condense is behavior-proven, not only argv-proven.
obs The lap also found the destaging hand from the peer's %291 amendment, and it is this seat: git pull --rebase --autostash creates and applies a stash EVEN WHEN already up to date, and stash apply restores changes UNSTAGED (no --index) -- so five reflex pulls this session each silently converted the peer's staged entries to unstaged, and one of their commits took 3 of 16 intended paths. The mechanism was messaged to the peer for their amendment while they held the ledger. The habit changes now: fetch first, pull --rebase only when actually behind, never as a reflex over a peer's staged work.
obs Holds and doors: the charter red still waits its booking lap (the ledger freed and refilled twice as the peer worked); the parity-witness seat for this family (rebuild all 135 strings via the fns and compare against elder extractions) is named for the round that wants a standing guard rather than a one-time proof; the tools/co room's next sameness is smaller and can wait.
